        <section class="section" id="questions">
          <span class="section-num">09</span>
          <div class="section-inner">
            <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">Common Questions · Farmington, Aztec &amp; Bloomfield</span></div>
            <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Foundation repair questions <em>tri-city homeowners ask</em></h2>
            <p class="reveal" style="max-width:680px;">Methods, costs, and what to expect — answered for this market specifically, not in general.</p>

            <div class="seo-accordion reveal">
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Slab repair Farmington NM</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Slab-on-grade construction is common across Farmington, Aztec, and Bloomfield, so slab repair is a frequent question in this market. The right approach depends on the mechanism: a slab settling into a soft or collapsible zone needs a different answer than a slab being lifted by expanding clay or shale. Those two can look similar from inside the house and require opposite responses.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Foundation repair cost Farmington, Aztec, Bloomfield</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Cost follows cause. A drainage correction and a pier system are separated by an order of magnitude, and both get recommended for the same set of symptoms by different companies. If estimates in this area vary wildly with no soil evaluation behind any of them, that spread is telling you about the companies, not the house.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Is Bloomfield's soil different from Farmington's?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Not as neatly as a town-by-town map would suggest. We've measured both heave and settlement in Bloomfield, and both show up in Farmington and Aztec too. The geology here varies at a finer scale than a city limit — which is exactly why an investigation at the specific address matters more than an assumption based on which town it's in.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Helical or push piers — which one for this area?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Both get used here, and the choice depends on what's actually under a given slab — the depth and consistency of bearing material, and whether the house is heaving or settling. Piers specified without that information are a design written on assumption rather than evidence.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Drainage correction in the tri-city area</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Where collapsible soil is present, keeping water away from the foundation is often the single most important intervention — collapsible soils are stable until sustained moisture reaches them for the first time, which can happen decades after construction. Where expansive clay or shale dominates, managing the moisture envelope around the foundation matters just as much, for the opposite reason.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Are cracked driveways and heaved garage floors a foundation problem?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Sometimes, sometimes not. Humped driveways and uneven garage floors in this area are a common, visible sign of expansive clay or shale activity nearby — but exterior concrete can move independently of the house itself. What matters is whether the pattern shows up inside the structure too, and whether it's progressing.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Do I need a soil investigation, or just a contractor?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Not every home needs one. Many questions in this area resolve with a careful look at drainage, grading, and the pattern of movement inside the house. A formal geotechnical investigation is warranted when the answer genuinely turns on what's happening at depth — and in those cases, the cost of the investigation is small against the cost of installing the wrong system. TLS works with <a href="https://www.sandiageo.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SandiaGEO</a> when a site reaches that threshold.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Foundation inspection in Farmington, Aztec, or Bloomfield — what happens?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">We look at exterior grading and drainage, the pattern and location of cracking inside and out, door and window operation, floor elevations where warranted, and the site's history — irrigation, past leaks, landscaping changes. Then we tell you what we think is happening and what, if anything, is worth doing about it. That last part sometimes ends with "nothing yet."</div></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

